# Nothing in this raw tree is ignored — every benchmark-corridor source is
# COMMITTED, including the fetched AEO2025 electricity-corridor CSV.
#
# History (D22, 2026-08-31): `aeo2025/` was previously ignored on the reasoning
# that the deterministic fetcher, not a hand-reproduced copy, is the rule-5-safe
# reproducible source. The fetcher IS still the source of the bytes — but
# ignoring the output broke the rubric §0 committed-artifacts contract: with
# `data/clean/` also gitignored (repo .gitignore §5), NO artifact in the chain
# was committed, so FC-5 had nothing to read and scored SKIPPED for every ISO
# with "no committed benchmark-corridor table". The "large fetched corpora
# (pjm-energy-offers)" precedent cited for the ignore does not apply: this file
# is 1,009 lines / 172 KB, four orders of magnitude below the corpora that rule
# exists for, and it needs a (rate-limited) EIA API key to regenerate — so
# ignoring it made the scoring input strictly less reproducible, not more.
#
# Committing it costs 172 KB and makes `curate_benchmark_corridor.py` runnable
# offline from committed raw, which is what the loader docstring already claims.
# Re-fetch to refresh: `python scripts/data/fetch_aeo_electricity.py`.
#
# The ISO planning WORKBOOKS are the one exception, and for the opposite reason:
# the ERCOT CDR is ~13 MB of XLSX. Those follow the corpus convention (CLAUDE.md
# "Cloning & session data"): the bulk payload is untracked, while the small
# machine-extracted unified CSV, the SHA256SUMS identity record and the README
# stay tracked — so curation still runs offline from committed raw. Recovery is
# re-fetch: `python scripts/data/fetch_iso_planning_benchmarks.py`.
ercot-cdr-2025/*.xlsx
pjm-load-2026/*.xlsx
